C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 Fall 2026
      Problem 7.1 from 
       
      Maximize profits from irrigation by optimizing the development and use of potential dams and water pump.
       
      Izzy Hinton
      isabel.hinton@usu.edu
      October 19, 2026
  11   
  12  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  13  SETS
  14  *Seasons are not actually stated as winter and spring. Just wanted to avoid confusion from using numbers in label
  15  src irrigation water sources /hd "high dam", ld "low dam", rd "river diversion"/
  16  time season that irrigation occurs in /w "winter", s "spring"/;
  17   
  18  * 2. DEFINE input data
  19  PARAMETERS
  20     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  21           /hd 10000,
  22            ld 6000,
  23            rd 8000/
  24     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  25           /hd 0,
  26            ld 0,
  27            rd 20/
  28    WaterDemand(time) volume of water required for an acre of crop (ac-ft per acre)
  29          /w 1,
  30           s 3/
  31  * river diversion capacity was given in ac-ft/day multiplying by 365/2 gives the semi-annual capacity
  32      Capacity(src) maximum volume of water that can be extraced from a source (ac-ft)
  33          /hd 700,
  34          ld 300,
  35          rd 401.5/
  36      Inflow(time) volume of water flowing into the reservoir each season (ac-ft)
  37          /w 600,
  38           s 200/
  39      Revenue rate of revenue produced from each irrigated acre ($ per acre) /300/
  40  *GroundWater is 2 ac-ft/day multiplied by 365/2 for the gw for 1 time step
  41      GroundWater volume of water that is added to the river flow through groundwater (ac-ft) /365/;
  42   
  43  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  44  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or develop project from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  45            X(src,time) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  46            TPROFIT total profit (revenue from irrigation minus cost to irrigate) ($);
  47   
  48  BINARY VARIABLES I;
  49   
  50  * Non-negativity constraints
  51  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  52   
  53  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  54  EQUATIONS
  55     TotalRev             Total revenue ($) and objective function value
  56     OneDam              Only one dam can be built
  57     MassBalanceRiver    Mass Balance for river diversion
  58     MassBalanceRes       Mass Balance for reservoir diversion
  59     CapacityConstraint  The water from each source must be less than its' maximum capacity;
  60   
  61  TotalRev..                 TPROFIT =E= SUM((src,time), Revenue*WaterDemand(time)*X(src,time))-Sum(src, CapCost(src)*I(src)) - SUM((src,time), OpCost(src)*X(src,time));
  62  OneDam..                         I("hd") + I("ld") =L= 1;
  63  MassBalanceRiver(time)..         X("rd", time) =L= Inflow(time) - X("hd",time) - X("ld", time) + GroundWater;
  64  MassBalanceRes(time)..           X("hd",time) + X("ld",time) =L= Inflow(time);
  65  CapacityConstraint(src,time)..    X(src,time) =L= Capacity(src)*I(src);
  66   
  67  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  68  MODEL IrrigationSources /ALL/;
  69   
  70  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  71  SOLVE IrrigationSources USING MIP MAXIMIZING TPROFIT;
  72   
  73   
  74   
  75   


